      "next_deadline": "No local capital NOFA; the Policy Board of the Housing for Health Partnership sets allocations. HHAP-7 planning runs through 2027. Contact Housing for Health about PSH operating or services funding tied to Coordinated Entry referrals.",
      "prevailing_wage_trigger": "false",
      "layering_notes": "Santa Cruz County housing programs sit in the Community Development and Infrastructure Department (CDI) Planning Division, Housing Section (HousingProgramsInfo@santacruzcountyCA.gov, (831) 454-2332), which runs the Measure J inclusionary program, the Affordable Housing Impact Fee, LMIHAF, PLHA and the County housing NOFA; the Human Services Department's Housing for Health Division (H4H, the CoC CA-508 lead) runs HHAP, Homekey and homeless programs; Behavioral Health holds BHSA; and the Housing Authority of the County of Santa Cruz (HACSC) supplies vouchers. The County is a non-entitlement jurisdiction for CDBG and HOME. County programs apply only to the unincorporated area (about half the population); Santa Cruz, Watsonville, Capitola and Scotts Valley run their own programs (Pass 3). cdi.santacruzcountyca.gov, santacruzcountyca.gov PDFs, santacruzhealth.org and housingforhealthpartnership.org open normally; the Board's IQM2 portal (santacruzcountyca.iqm2.com) renders by script and could not be listed, so Board items were taken from NOFA appendices and press coverage. HHAP underwrites the services side of PSH projects such as Veterans Village and Park Haven Plaza; it has not funded capital. The December 2025 Policy Board packet also records a CoC decision to prioritize HUD CoC rental assistance for PSH projects threatened by federal NOFO changes. Source is the Housing for Health Partnership Policy Board packet of December 17, 2025; HHAP-5 figure from the County's February 2024 press release and HHAP-6 and HHAP-7 figures from HCD allocation tables.",
